s rebuilt on that outline (fit 21% -> 66%; the rest is the bridge over the water and the square, which the outlines leave empty), and the plate widened to 360 m to hold the river. Busmann + Haberer's building (1986): dark red brick over a stone base, tall slit windows, the roofline of curved zinc sheds rising to north-light glazing in ranks across the whole length, the higher stair and lift tower on the square and the tower toward the cathedral, the low foyer range along the south with a planted roof, the terrace block stepping down toward the Rhine garden, the glass entrance porch; Heinrich-Boell-Platz over the concert hall, paved, with a hint of Dani Karavan's Ma'alot; the viaduct with its arches and tracks; the bridge's bowstring arch over the river (one of its three spans); the Rhine garden, the road and the promenade's avenue of plane trees; the block across Am Domhof to the south. Guessed: heights (17 m to the eaves, sheds 7 m), the sheds' count and direction, where the slit windows fall, the towers. The cathedral and the Roman-Germanic Museum lie just off the plate to the west."}
